67 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (TCampaign finance remains one of the most under-regulated dimensions of India's electoral ecosystem. Unregulated money flows distort democratic outcomes, entrench political inequality, and erode public trust in electoral institutions. India's existing framework, while evolving, continues to face critical challenges around disclosure norms, enforcement gaps, and the influence of undisclosed funding. Modernizing the electoral ecosystem demands a comprehensive reimagining of campaign finance laws, digital disclosure mechanisms, and the institutional role of election management bodies. This conference positions itself at the centre of that urgent reform conversation.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
68 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (Election bodies worldwide have introduced innovative campaign finance reforms. Countries like Canada and Brazil use real-time expense disclosure systems. Germany and Sweden follow strong public funding models. AI-based monitoring and open-data dashboards have improved transparency globally. While Election Commission of India has made important progress, enforcement gaps remain. This conference explores global best practices adaptable to the Indian democratic system.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
69 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (This conference aims to generate evidence-based, actionable insights on campaign finance regulation to inform policy, legal reform, and institutional practice. It seeks to build a structured multi-stakeholder dialogue among legal scholars, electoral administrators, and the academic community. A core goal is to expose students and research scholars to comparative international perspectives otherwise inaccessible in conventional academic settings. The conference further aspires to produce documented deliberations serving as a reference for future reform advocacy, contributing to the constitutional vision of free and fair elections.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
